{%- comment -%}
  Klarna "Pay in 3" line for the buy column and the product card.

  This renders only when Klarna is genuinely switched on for the shop, read
  from shop.enabled_payment_types — the same list that drives the payment icon
  row. An instalment offer the customer cannot actually take at checkout is a
  promise broken at the worst possible moment, so the page is not allowed to
  make one on its own. Turn Klarna on in Settings > Payments and every one of
  these lines appears; turn it off and they all go.

  Klarna's UK Pay in 3 also has an order floor and ceiling. Outside that range
  Klarna declines the split, so the line stays hidden rather than quoting an
  instalment the shopper would never be offered.

  On a product page the figure has to follow the variant the shopper picks: a
  £29.67 instalment sitting under a £169 price is a wrong number, not a stale
  one. The element carries its bounds as data attributes and pl-pdp-plus.js
  recomputes it from the displayed price, hiding it on a variant Klarna would
  not split. Pass `product` as well and the line is rendered for any product
  with at least one splittable variant, so switching to that variant can
  reveal it even when the one loaded first was out of range.

  Required
    price      variant or product price, in pence

  Optional
    product    the product, so a splittable variant can reveal the line
    min        lowest order Klarna will split, pence. Default 3500.
    max        highest order Klarna will split, pence. Default 100000.
    compact    short form for product cards. Default false.
    force      render even when Klarna is not detected. Only for a provider
               that does not register a payment type of its own.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign amount = price | default: 0 | plus: 0
  assign floor_p = min | default: 3500 | plus: 0
  assign ceil_p = max | default: 100000 | plus: 0

  assign live = false
  for t in shop.enabled_payment_types
    assign tn = t | downcase
    if tn contains 'klarna'
      assign live = true
    endif
  endfor
  if force
    assign live = true
  endif

  comment
    Klarna puts any remainder in the first instalment, so the split is never
    below this figure. Rounding up keeps the quoted number one the shopper
    will actually see rather than one that is a penny short.
  endcomment
  assign per = amount | plus: 2 | divided_by: 3

  assign in_range = false
  if amount >= floor_p and amount <= ceil_p
    assign in_range = true
  endif

  assign any_range = in_range
  if product
    for var in product.variants
      if var.price >= floor_p and var.price <= ceil_p
        assign any_range = true
      endif
    endfor
  endif

  assign ok = false
  if live and any_range
    assign ok = true
  endif
-%}
{%- if ok -%}
  {%- if compact -%}
    <span class="pl-bnpl pl-bnpl--compact">3 &times; {{ per | money }} with Klarna</span>
  {%- else -%}
    <p class="pl-bnpl" data-pl-bnpl data-min="{{ floor_p }}" data-max="{{ ceil_p }}"{% unless in_range %} hidden{% endunless %}>
      <span class="pl-bnpl__mark" aria-hidden="true">Klarna</span>
      <span>Or 3 interest-free payments of <b data-pl-bnpl-amt>{{ per | money }}</b>. Chosen at checkout.</span>
    </p>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
